1875, 0.0166168212890625, 0.0237884521484375, -0.0175628662109375, 0.01088714599609375, 0.003986358642578125, -0.0239410400390625, -0.0009984970092773438, -0.034027099609375, -0.0189208984375, -0.01873779296875, -0.0126190185546875, 0.013824462890625, -0.01465606689453125, -0.01380157470703125, -0.005828857421875, 0.006710052490234375, -0.00884246826171875, -0.0135345458984375, 0.01505279541015625, 0.05413818359375, -0.03173828125, -0.006473541259765625, -0.0006031990051269531, 0.01395416259765625, -0.006717681884765625, 0.0055389404296875, 0.01021575927734375, -0.00461578369140625, -0.004825592041015625, -0.03582763671875, -0.016357421875, -0.0174560546875, -0.018707275390625, 0.03668212890625, -0.0232086181640625, 0.0141448974609375, -0.066162109375, -0.00782012939453125, -0.01529693603515625, -0.006290435791015625, 0.00022864341735839844, -0.0168914794921875, -0.01119232177734375, -0.00563812255859375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "This short work extends the permanental process model to include fixed effects, providing a framework that enhances interpretability and flexibility. The permanental process is a type of stochastic process used for modeling random point patterns, while fixed effects refer to parameters that capture systematic, non-random influences in the data. Our extension allows for both structured (fixed) and random components in the intensity function, which is particularly useful in scientific applications where certain covariates are known to have a direct effect.\n\nWe begin by placing a prior distribution on the fixed effects coefficients. Specifically, we consider a Gaussian prior with large variance, often referred to as a \"diffuse prior,\" which reflects minimal prior information about the coefficients. In the limit as the prior variance tends to infinity, the prior becomes non-informative, allowing the data to dominate the estimation of fixed effects.\n\nUsing the representer theorem\u2014a result from the theory of Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Spaces (RKHS) that characterizes the form of solutions to certain regularized optimization problems\u2014we show that the intensity function of the permanental process can be decomposed into two components: a fixed effects term and a function belonging to an RKHS. This decomposition clarifies the roles of both fixed and random effects in the model. The limiting equivalent kernel, which arises in this context, defines an RKHS whose squared norm corresponds exactly to the limiting penalty imposed by the prior, providing a direct connection between the probabilistic and functional analytic perspectives.\n\nThis framework allows for straightforward scientific interpretation: for example, fixed effects can represent known environmental or experimental factors, while the RKHS component captures complex, unknown structure in the data. Domain knowledge can be incorporated by specifying the form of the fixed effects or by choosing an appropriate kernel for the RKHS, thus tailoring the model to the scientific context at hand.\n\nIn summary, our extension of the permanental process model provides a principled way to include fixed effects, leverages the representer theorem for efficient computation, and facilitates the incorporation of domain knowledge. For readers seeking further background, we refer to Macchi (1975) for permanental processes, Wahba (1990) for RKHS and the representer theorem, and standard texts on mixed models for fixed effects."}}